title Decline thirty-six, three divergences and a lost race on one send
prompt Run the next incense lap per recursion-prompts/incense-inner.md's rite and current order.
think Ran round-open; clean. Checked the claim board against construction/REDS.md, construction/ITINERARY.md, and .claude/rules -- live claims present, none overlapping the law lane, confirmed fresh at every recheck across the lap.
think Diffed git log against the prior lap's checked HEAD at each recheck and found no commit touching construction/REDS.md across sixteen intervening commits.
think Attempted git push xy main four times. The first two attempts were rejected by true divergence, each recovered by fleet_round_open.sh parking the two rejected commits whole onto a named pier/diverged-* branch (already pushed, nothing lost) and resetting main to the fetched tip.
think The third attempt lost the race but re-derived cleanly -- round-open rebased the lap's two commits onto the new upstream tip with no conflict, and the amend convention (rule 2) carried the ITINERARY nib field forward onto the rebased commit.
think The fourth push attempt was rejected again, this time a true divergence, parked onto a third pier/diverged-* branch.
think Re-checked the claim board and the REDS diff fresh against the confirmed HEAD one more time before writing this final account.
obs A hot pier can cost several retries in a row on the same send -- two true divergences, one clean rebase, and a third true divergence, all on one lap's own commit pair -- and the fix each time is identical: round-open, recheck fresh, rewrite, retry.
obs A trivial zero-impact decline record is the right lap to absorb that retry cost; a lap carrying real work would want the same discipline without the extra turns this lap spent proving it.
file recursion-prompts/incense-inner.md why final lap record for the thirty-sixth decline, naming the pier traffic as the lap's own finding.
recommend keep-going -- the law lane waits on a human word at %642 or %519; nothing in the ledger or the claim board changed this lap beyond heavy ordinary peer traffic.
rota lap 41, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels. The felt thing this lap was the pier's own weather: four send attempts, three stumbles, one clean landing.
scope captain's round-open, claim-board, and REDS-diff check for the law lane; three recovered divergences and one clean rebase on a single send; no code or ledger change.
status GREEN -- the decline record lands once the final send clears; claim board and REDS diff reconfirmed fresh immediately before this write.
